WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.620
What gives, Mickey?

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:05.680
Disney+ subscribers awoke on New Year's Day
to find that the brand new streaming service

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.800
had removed several movies from its library.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.960
Needless to say, these subscribers were less
than pleased.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.050
The deletions are surprising for a couple
of reasons.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.740
First, everything on Disney+ is owned by the
Walt Disney Corporation.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.050
With none of its content licensed from other
parties, there doesn't appear to be any reason

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.880
for anything to ever disappear from the streamer's
library.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.900
In fact, many subscribers were under the impression
that the Disney+ library would not be rotating

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.569
out titles for any reason.

00:00:31.569 --> 00:00:35.559
For another, the fact that content has begun
to disappear so soon after the streamer's

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.760
November 14 launch is surprising.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.560
While the list of the vanished titles isn't
very extensive, the Disney+ library wasn't

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:47.200
super-deep to begin with, and the deletions
appear to have taken place for no good reason.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.239
Perhaps the most glaring omission from the
Disney+ library is the 1990 classic Home Alone,

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.400
which a truckload of subscribers are likely
to have watched mere days before it vanished.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.780
It is, after all, a Christmas classic.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.410
We suppose it stands to reason that it may
only appear on the streamer seasonally.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.680
If that’s the case, however, Disney should
have informed its subscribers that some titles

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.240
would only be available at certain times of
the year.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.080
The flick's 1992 sequel also disappeared,
although 1997's Home Alone 3, which features

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:19.331
an entirely different cast and is nowhere
near as well-regarded as the first two, is

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:20.140
still available.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.560
In addition to those films, the offerings
that have mysteriously left the service are:

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.820
Dr. Dolittle, Flicka, Pirates of the Caribbean:
On Stranger Tides, Strange Magic, The Wonderful

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.651
World of Disney: Magical Holiday Celebration,
White Wilderness, Mickey, Donald, Goofy: The

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:40.340
Three Musketeers, Garfield 2: A Tail of Two
Kitties, the 2006 remake of The Shaggy Dog,

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:40.840
and The Sandlot.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.360
To make matters worse, Disney has had no public
statement to offer about the situation as

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.180
of this video.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.500
The only possible clue has come from Reddit,
where a single user was reportedly told by

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.580
customer service that the deletions weren't
intentional, but were actually a technical

00:01:58.580 --> 00:01:59.080
issue.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.960
If it were a mere technical problem, however,
it seems like Disney would have said something

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.440
publicly about it by now.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.220
In fact, the Mouse House's complete radio
silence isn't helping matters.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:13.040
Disney+ was already looking at the loss of
a not-insignificant number of subscribers

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.220
now that the first season of its original
Star Wars series The Mandalorian has concluded.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:21.640
To follow that up by deleting random titles
from its library doesn't seem like a great

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.460
way to reassure anyone that the service is
worth keeping.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.650
You may be wondering, with all of the titles
that have suddenly departed, if Disney+ will

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.920
be adding anything new to its library this
month.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.760
Perhaps unsurprisingly, the service will be
adding plenty of new titles.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.940
Most significantly, last year's live-action
remakes of Aladdin and The Lion King will

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.540
be streaming on the 8th and 28th, respectively.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:47.340
Already available as of January 1 are the
2003 classic Holes, the 1993 sports comedy

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:53.020
Cool Runnings, and the fascinating 2014 documentary
Marvel: 75 Years, From Pulp to Pop!

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.680
Also arriving at various points during the
month will be new episodes of Disney+ original

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:03.240
series Forky Asks a Question, Encore!, High
School Musical: The Musical: The Series, Marvel's

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:08.790
Hero Project, Pixar in Real Life, Disney Family
Sundays, and The World According to Jeff Goldblum.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:13.290
Also, the third and final season of Marvel's
Runaways will hit the platform on January

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:18.550
10, and a wealth of entire seasons of Disney
Channel and NatGeo series such as The Lodge,

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:22.460
The Proud Family, and Dog: Impossible will
also be making their debuts.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.819
It stands to reason, though, that subscribers
would like some reassurance that their favorite

00:03:26.819 --> 00:03:31.220
content isn't going to suddenly disappear
from Disney+ at some point down the road.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:35.130
We'll be keeping a vigilant eye out for any
statement from the House of Mouse, and we'll

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:38.030
update you as soon as any new information
becomes available.

